// Design 130 accessor rule (M3): `String.substring` panics on a range outside
// the string. It used to CLAMP — `"hello".substring(-9, 99)` returned the whole
// string and `"hello".substring(5, 2)` returned `""` — so a caller could not
// distinguish a bug from a legitimately empty slice.
// EXPECT: panic
// DF-249a: a RANGE accessor spells both bounds, so the message shows the whole
// range that was asked for beside the length it had to fit in.
// EXPECT-PANIC-CONTAINS: String.substring: range out of range: -9..99 (len 5)

func main() {
    print("hello".substring(1, 3))    // "el" — in range
    print("hello".substring(2, 2))    // "" — an empty range is legal
    print("hello".substring(-9, 99))  // out of bounds -> panic
}
